        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128203; WHAT YOU NEED FOR THIS LESSON</div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px; border-left: 3px solid #D0D9E8; padding-left: 14px;"><span style="color: #c7922c; font-size: 18px; flex-shrink: 0;">&#9654;</span>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.6;">This page.</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 1 &middot; THE COMANCHE</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">The People of the Horse</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The Comanche call themselves <strong>the People</strong> (<a href="#foreign" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="NOO-moo-noo. The Comanche name for themselves. It means the People."><em>Numunuu</em></a>). Long before Texas was a state, they lived in the Rocky Mountains. In the early 1700s, they moved south onto the open southern Plains. By then, they had horses. Spanish settlers had brought horses to North America, and the animals spread north through trade and theft.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The horse changed everything. A rider could follow a bison herd across hundreds of miles, and a rider could strike a camp or a ranch and be gone before help arrived. The Comanche became some of the finest horsemen the world has ever seen. They pushed out the Apache and other peoples and took control of a huge region. Spanish speakers called that region <a href="#foreign" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="koh-man-cheh-REE-uh. The name Spanish speakers gave to the Comanche homeland on the southern Plains."><em>Comanchería</em></a>. It covered much of West Texas and reached into what are now Oklahoma, Kansas, and New Mexico.</p><div id="foreign" style="background: #E8F6FB; border-left: 5px solid #2196D0; border-top: 3px solid #55C8E8; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 20px; margin: 14px 0;"><div style="font-size: 11px; color: #1B6A94; margin-bottom: 8px;">&#128161; FOREIGN LANGUAGE VOCABULARY</div><div style="font-size: 16px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.55; margin-bottom: 6px;"><strong>Comanchero</strong><button type="button" class="gloss-audio" aria-label="Play pronunciation of Comanchero" onclick="speakGloss(event,'koh man chair oh')">&#128266;</button> <span style="color: #6b7a99;">(koh-man-CHAIR-oh)</span>: A New Mexican trader who traveled onto the Plains to trade with the Comanche.</div><div style="font-size: 16px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.55; margin-bottom: 6px;"><strong>Comanchería</strong><button type="button" class="gloss-audio" aria-label="Play pronunciation of Comanchería" onclick="speakGloss(event,'koh man cheh ree uh')">&#128266;</button> <span style="color: #6b7a99;">(koh-man-cheh-REE-uh)</span>: The name Spanish speakers gave to the Comanche homeland on the southern Plains.</div><div style="font-size: 16px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.55; margin-bottom: 6px;"><strong>Numunuu</strong><button type="button" class="gloss-audio" aria-label="Play pronunciation of Numunuu" onclick="speakGloss(event,'noo moo noo')">&#128266;</button> <span style="color: #6b7a99;">(NOO-moo-noo)</span>: The Comanche name for themselves. It means the People.</div></div><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The Comanche were not one army under one king. They were a family of <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A group of families who live, travel, and make decisions together.">band</a>s. Each band chose its own leaders, who were followed because people respected them. No single chief could speak for all. This is one reason treaties with the Comanche were so hard. A promise by one band did not bind another. The southernmost band, the Penateka, lived closest to Texans, in the hills and plains west of Austin and San Antonio. Other bands, such as the Nokoni, Kotsoteka, Yamparika, and Quahadi, lived farther north and west.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 2 &middot; HOW THEY LIVED AND FOUGHT</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Bison, Trade, and Raiding</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The bison gave the Comanche nearly everything they needed. They ate its meat. They made tents, clothing, and tools from its hides and bones. They traded the extra hides, and they traded horses. For years, Spanish and then Mexican officials in New Mexico and Texas treated the Comanche as a power to be dealt with, not a people to be conquered.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The Comanche also made war and took what they wanted. A <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A sudden attack made to take horses, cattle, goods, or captives.">raid</a> on a ranch or village in Texas or northern Mexico could bring horses, cattle, and goods. It could also bring captives. Some captives were adopted into Comanche families, some were traded, and some were ransomed back. Warriors won honor in such raids, and wealth. The reach was long. In 1840, riders had come all the way to the Gulf coast at Linnville, and they burned the town. The Comanche had the strength to do that when Texas was an independent republic. They were still strong after Texas became a state.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Comanche raiders also had friends. <a href="#foreign" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="koh-man-CHAIR-oh. A New Mexican trader who traveled onto the Plains to trade with the Comanche."><em>Comanchero</em></a>s, traders from New Mexico, rode out onto the Plains to trade bread, tools, and cloth for horses and cattle. Many of those animals had been taken in raids. That trade helped keep the Comanche economy running.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">When you read about the Comanche, avoid two simple stories. One says they were only cruel raiders. The other says they were only victims. The truth is that they were a rich and powerful people whose way of life depended on horses, bison, trade, and war. Texans who met them had good reason to fear them, and the Comanche had good reason to defend their land.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-select" data-ra-skip>
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; TRY IT &middot; CAUSE AND RESULT</div>
  <div class="widget-question">Each row names something about Comanche life. Pick what followed from it.</div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="1" data-fb="Right. A rider could follow a bison herd for hundreds of miles, or strike a camp and be gone before help arrived." data-hint="Think about how far a rider can travel compared with a person on foot.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">1. The Comanche got horses.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">They stopped hunting bison</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">They could follow bison and strike far from home</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">They moved back to the mountains</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="2" data-fb="Right. A promise by one band did not bind another, which is one reason treaties were so hard." data-hint="Think about what a treaty needs: someone who can promise for everyone.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">2. No single chief could speak for all the Comanche bands.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">Treaties were easy to make</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Every band followed the Penateka</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">A promise by one band did not bind the others</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="0" data-fb="Right. Many of those animals had been taken in raids, so the trade helped keep the Comanche economy running." data-hint="Ask where many of the horses and cattle came from.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">3. Comancheros brought bread, tools and cloth onto the Plains to trade for horses and cattle.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The trade helped keep raiding and the Comanche economy going</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">The trade ended the raiding</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">The trade made the Comanche poor</button></div>
  </div>
  <button class="widget-reset">Reset</button>
</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 3 &middot; TEXAS MEETS THE PLAINS</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Settlers Press the Line</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">In 1850, Texas had about 212,000 people. By 1860, it had about 604,000. The number nearly tripled in ten years. Many newcomers came from the older Southern states. They came to farm cotton, and some brought enslaved people. German settlers built towns such as New Braunfels and Fredericksburg in the Hill Country. Every new farm and town moved the line of settlement farther west, toward Comanche hunting grounds.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">When Texas joined the Union, the United States took responsibility for defending the frontier. The army built a first line of forts in 1848 and 1849, running south from the Red River to the Rio Grande. As settlers pushed farther west, it built a second line in the 1850s. But the forts were far apart, and soldiers on foot could rarely catch Comanche riders. Texans often felt the army was not doing enough. They argued that Texas should defend itself, and so the Texas Rangers stayed in the picture.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Some leaders tried peace. In 1847, John O. Meusebach, who led the German settlers, met Penateka leaders in the Hill Country. They made a <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A written agreement between nations or peoples.">treaty</a>. The Comanche agreed to let the Germans settle and travel on certain lands, and the Germans promised gifts. Texans told for years afterward how well it was kept. But it covered only the Penateka and a small area. Other bands were not part of it.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The Penateka were also in trouble from within. A cholera epidemic in 1849 killed many Comanche. War and settlement pushed them out of their old lands. In the 1850s, a few hundred of them moved to a small <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="Land set aside by a government for Native people to live on.">reservation</a> on the Clear Fork of the Brazos River. Texas had kept its public lands when it joined the Union, so the federal government could not simply set aside reservations, and the state agreed to a small amount. Settlers nearby complained that raids still happened, and they blamed the reservation Indians. In 1859, angry Texans threatened the reservations, and federal officials moved the Native people north to Indian Territory.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">So by 1860, the frontier had not closed. Settlers had come farther west, but the Comanche remained a power on the plains beyond them. Then the Civil War pulled the army out of Texas, and the story took a new turn.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-seq" data-ra-skip data-next="0" data-done="By 1860 the frontier had not closed. The Comanche remained a power on the plains beyond the settlers." data-hint="Not yet. Which of these came earlier?">
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; TRY IT &middot; TEXAS MEETS THE PLAINS</div>
  <div class="widget-question">These five events show how Texans and the Comanche met on the frontier. Click them in the order they happened.</div>
  <div class="seq-msg"></div>
  <div class="seq-pool"><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="3" data-date="The 1850s" data-text="A few hundred Penateka move to a small reservation on the Clear Fork" data-fb="It sat on the Brazos River.">A few hundred Penateka move to a small reservation on the Clear Fork</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="0" data-date="1840" data-text="Comanche riders reach the Gulf coast and burn Linnville" data-fb="They struck when Texas was still an independent republic.">Comanche riders reach the Gulf coast and burn Linnville</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="4" data-date="1859" data-text="Federal officials move the reservation peoples north" data-fb="Angry Texans had threatened the reservations.">Federal officials move the reservation peoples north</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="2" data-date="1848 and 1849" data-text="The army builds its first line of forts" data-fb="The line ran south from the Red River to the Rio Grande.">The army builds its first line of forts</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="1" data-date="1847" data-text="Meusebach makes a treaty with Penateka leaders" data-fb="It covered only the Penateka and a small area.">Meusebach makes a treaty with Penateka leaders</button></div>
  <div class="seq-line"></div>
  <div class="widget-feedback"></div>
  <button class="widget-reset">Reset</button>
</div>
    </div>

    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 12px;">&#128170; WHAT TO CARRY FORWARD</div>
        <p style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0;">A people who could defend a huge region for a hundred years were not a footnote to Texas history. They were one of its main characters. As you read on, ask what each side wanted, and what each side could actually do to get it.</p>
    </div>
